# Primary Key

## Simple Definition

A Primary Key uniquely identifies a row.

Properties:

- Unique
- Cannot be NULL
- One Primary Key per table

---

## Example

Menu Table

| product_id | product_name |
|------------|-------------|
| 1 | Sushi |
| 2 | Curry |
| 3 | Ramen |

Primary Key:

```text
product_id
```

---

# Foreign Key

## Simple Definition

A Foreign Key references a Primary Key from another table.

---

## Example

Sales Table

| product_id |
|------------|
| 1 |
| 2 |
| 3 |

References:

```text
Menu.product_id
```

Relationship:

```text
Sales.product_id (FK)
            |
            |
            V
Menu.product_id (PK)
```

---

# Join Cardinality

Cardinality describes how tables are related.

---

# 1:1 Relationship

One row matches one row.

Example:

Employee ↔ Passport

```text
Employee
   1
   |
   |
   1
Passport
```

---

# 1:M Relationship

Most common relationship.

Customer ↔ Orders

```text
Customer
    1
    |
    |
    M
Orders
```

One customer can place many orders.

---

# M:1 Relationship

Reverse of 1:M.

```text
Orders
   M
   |
   |
   1
Customer
```

Many orders belong to one customer.

---

# M:M Relationship

Students and Courses.

```text
Students
    M
    |
    |
    M
Courses
```

Requires a bridge table.

