# Why Duplicate Rows Appear After JOIN?

Very common interview question.

---

# Example

Customers

| customer_id |
|-------------|
| A |

Orders

| order_id | customer_id |
|----------|-------------|
| 1 | A |
| 2 | A |
| 3 | A |

---

# Query

```sql
SELECT *
FROM Customers c
INNER JOIN Orders o
ON c.customer_id = o.customer_id;
```

---

# Output

| customer_id | order_id |
|-------------|----------|
| A | 1 |
| A | 2 |
| A | 3 |

---

# Why?

Customer A has 3 matching rows.

SQL returns all matches.

This is expected behavior.

---

# Father-Children Story

```text
Father = Rahul

Child 1
Child 2
Child 3
```

JOIN Result

```text
Rahul Child1
Rahul Child2
Rahul Child3
```

Father repeats because multiple matches exist.

